1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,104
On Wednesday morning, the United States
Army blew up five tons of a chemical

2
00:00:04,134 --> 00:00:06,117
that was ready to explode on its own.

3
00:00:06,367 --> 00:00:10,634
It did it on purpose, right next to a town
of about four hundred people.

4
00:00:10,884 --> 00:00:15,053
Stay to the end, because the Army says the
air is clean, and one question

5
00:00:15,083 --> 00:00:16,204
still has no answer.

6
00:00:16,454 --> 00:00:19,514
It starts in a place most Americans have
never heard of.

7
00:00:19,544 --> 00:00:20,452
Middletown, Iowa.

8
00:00:20,702 --> 00:00:24,874
Next to the town sits the Iowa Army
Ammunition Plant, a huge site that has

9
00:00:24,904 --> 00:00:27,146
made munitions since nineteen forty-one.

10
00:00:27,396 --> 00:00:30,129
For years, nobody outside the fence had a
reason to worry.

11
00:00:30,379 --> 00:00:33,441
Then, on March twelfth, the whole plant
suddenly shut down.

12
00:00:33,691 --> 00:00:37,890
The Army called it a proactive safety
review, and said almost nothing else.

13
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:40,084
A week later, the reason came out.

14
00:00:40,114 --> 00:00:41,710
A chemical called lead azide.

15
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,178
Lead azide is a very sensitive explosive.

16
00:00:44,208 --> 00:00:47,776
It is used to set off other explosives in
shells and cartridges.

17
00:00:48,026 --> 00:00:50,292
And the Army had sixty-eight barrels of
it.

18
00:00:50,322 --> 00:00:52,215
Close to ninety-five hundred pounds.

19
00:00:52,465 --> 00:00:54,468
It had been stored there for more than
twenty years.

20
00:00:54,718 --> 00:00:55,795
Here is the problem.

21
00:00:55,825 --> 00:01:00,003
Over time, the material had turned back
into crystals, and that made it more

22
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:01,774
sensitive to friction and shock.

23
00:01:02,024 --> 00:01:03,591
You cannot just truck it away.

24
00:01:03,621 --> 00:01:05,028
Moving it could set it off.

25
00:01:05,278 --> 00:01:07,910
So the Army had a choice, and none of the
choices were good.

26
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,285
Its answer was to set it off, under
control, on site.

27
00:01:11,535 --> 00:01:13,656
In November, the Army announced the plan.

28
00:01:13,686 --> 00:01:16,803
Two detonations, the first one set for
Friday the twentieth.

29
00:01:17,053 --> 00:01:20,584
The blast would send a plume into the air,
with some lead particles in it.

30
00:01:20,834 --> 00:01:24,602
The Army's own modeling said the lead in
the air might rise, but stay well

31
00:01:24,632 --> 00:01:25,988
below the federal standard.

32
00:01:26,018 --> 00:01:27,374
That is what the Army says.

33
00:01:27,624 --> 00:01:31,779
Critics said the dust could travel close
to one hundred miles, and settle on

34
00:01:31,809 --> 00:01:33,321
soil, playgrounds, and cars.

35
00:01:33,571 --> 00:01:37,202
Health officials told people nearby to
stay indoors for up to two days after

36
00:01:37,232 --> 00:01:37,684
the blast.

37
00:01:37,714 --> 00:01:40,671
And they reminded everyone that there is
no safe level of lead.

38
00:01:40,921 --> 00:01:44,277
The biggest worry was small children, who
touch everything and put their hands

39
00:01:44,307 --> 00:01:44,971
in their mouths.

40
00:01:45,221 --> 00:01:48,001
Environmental groups went to court to stop
it.

41
00:01:48,031 --> 00:01:51,849
They argued the emergency permit should
never have been issued.

42
00:01:52,099 --> 00:01:55,665
The date slipped from Friday, to Monday,
and then to Wednesday.

43
00:01:55,915 --> 00:01:58,106
Residents said they felt kept in the dark.

44
00:01:58,356 --> 00:02:02,770
Then, Wednesday, around nine in the
morning, it happened.

45
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:03,940
Two explosions.

46
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:06,747
Two hazy plumes rose over the plant.

47
00:02:06,997 --> 00:02:11,603
That was the nation's entire stockpile of
this chemical, gone in a few seconds.

48
00:02:11,853 --> 00:02:17,097
Within hours, federal officials said their
monitors were reading zero lead in

49
00:02:17,127 --> 00:02:17,652
the air.

50
00:02:17,682 --> 00:02:19,873
In Iowa, and in nearby Illinois.

51
00:02:20,123 --> 00:02:22,910
By the evening, the Army gave residents
the all-clear.

52
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:27,994
Army leaders called it a huge success, and
told the public not to panic.

53
00:02:28,244 --> 00:02:29,658
So, case closed?

54
00:02:29,688 --> 00:02:30,561
Not quite.

55
00:02:30,811 --> 00:02:33,161
The monitors measure what floats in the
air.

56
00:02:33,191 --> 00:02:36,027
The real worry was the dust that falls to
the ground.

57
00:02:36,277 --> 00:02:40,684
Officials say monitoring will continue,
but they have given few details about

58
00:02:40,714 --> 00:02:42,758
how far it will go, or for how long.

59
00:02:43,008 --> 00:02:47,069
Residents are still being told to rinse
off outdoor surfaces, wipe down play

60
00:02:47,099 --> 00:02:48,737
equipment, and wash their cars.

61
00:02:48,987 --> 00:02:51,522
That is the question nobody has answered
yet.

62
00:02:51,552 --> 00:02:54,143
What is on the ground in Middletown right
now?

63
00:02:54,393 --> 00:02:55,253
Would you have stayed home?
